# Stage 20D2B reverse-proxy reference configuration (docs/remote-management.md
# §17/§20). This is operator-applied guidance, not something this repository's
# own tooling installs, configures, or starts. Replace stream.example.com
# with your own domain, and set STREAMING_TREE_REMOTE_MANAGEMENT_ORIGIN to
# exactly "https://stream.example.com" (or with an explicit port if you use
# one) to match.
#
# This is the ONLY supported D2B proxy topology: exactly one reverse-proxy
# hop, on the same host as the Streaming Tree backend, proxying only to its
# loopback listener. Do not chain this behind a second proxy/CDN, and do not
# run it on a different host - docs/remote-management.md §6/§7 explain why.
#
# Caddy automatically obtains and renews a certificate for the domain named
# in the site block (ACME/Let's Encrypt) - no certificate paths need to be
# configured here.
#
# PRE-20D2C correction (docs/remote-management.md §17): an earlier version
# of this file used a single bare `reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080` with no
# matcher. Confirmed directly against Caddy's own current documentation -
# "If the matcher token is omitted, it is the same as a wildcard matcher
# (*)" - a bare reverse_proxy forwards EVERY path, including /overlay/* and
# /api/public/*, contradicting this project's own stated policy that the
# D2B management origin does not expose the local-only public-overlay
# surface. The `handle` directive below is Caddy's own documented pattern
# for exactly this "these specific paths get one behavior, everything else
# gets another" case - handle blocks are mutually exclusive, evaluated in
# match-specificity order regardless of where they appear textually in the
# file (Caddy's own directive-sorting behavior, not directive-declaration
# order), so this is correct regardless of which handle block is written
# first.

stream.example.com {
	# The local-only public-overlay/Browser-Source surface
	# (/overlay/*, served by the embedded frontend's own client-side
	# router) and the backend's own intentionally-unauthenticated
	# public API (/api/public/*, the same routes /overlay/* pages call)
	# are NEVER forwarded through this management origin. Both remain
	# reachable only from the machine's own loopback interface - the
	# same as every other mode this application has - never through
	# this proxy. Stage 20D2C (docs/remote-ingest.md) exposes them
	# instead through a SEPARATE capability origin (never this one, and
	# never sharing this origin's management session cookie - see
	# docs/examples/Caddyfile.self-hosted for that combined reference).
	#
	# Stage 20D2C correction (docs/remote-ingest.md §1/§14): Caddy's own
	# matcher documentation confirms `/foo/*` does NOT match the bare
	# `/foo` (no trailing segment) - so a wildcard-only matcher here
	# left the exact roots `/overlay` and `/api/public` unexcluded.
	# Both exact roots are listed explicitly alongside the wildcards
	# below so this boundary does not depend on the backend's own
	# router happening to 404 an exact root today.
	@excludedLocalOnlySurface {
		path /overlay /overlay/* /api/public /api/public/*
	}
	handle @excludedLocalOnlySurface {
		respond 404
	}

	# Everything else - the management SPA, its hashed static assets,
	# /legal/*, /api/health, /api/auth/*, and every authenticated
	# management /api/* route - is proxied to the loopback backend.
	# Caddy overwrites (never trusts/passes through) client-supplied
	# X-Forwarded-For/X-Forwarded-Proto/X-Forwarded-Host headers by
	# default - confirmed directly against Caddy's own reverse_proxy
	# documentation (docs/remote-management.md §2) - so no additional
	# header configuration is required for the application's own
	# forwarding-header contract (docs/remote-management.md §8) to
	# hold.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
	}
}

# Do NOT add a second site block or handle_path directive proxying to:
#   - the MediaMTX Control API (loopback-only, never proxied - Stage 20D2C
#     remote ingest does not exist yet);
#   - the MediaMTX RTMP listener (loopback-only, never proxied).
# Do NOT remove the @excludedLocalOnlySurface handle block above merely to
# simplify this file - it is the actual security boundary between "the D2B
# management origin" and "the local-only public-overlay surface", not a
# redundant example.
